-- MRCP Part 1 -- Cardiology Learn chapters (16), matching the mockup
-- approved for the Cardiology specialty. Original writing; the
-- 'arrhythmias' chapter was checked against two StatPearls/NCBI
-- Bookshelf sources (see its refs column). status='published' per
-- explicit instruction, same as the question-bank content batches.

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Cardiac arrhythmias$t$,
  'arrhythmias',
  $t$Two mechanisms, a focus that fires too fast, or a circuit that never stops, explain almost every rhythm disturbance tested at this level.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>An umbrella term for abnormalities of heart rate or rhythm arising from disorders of impulse generation (automaticity) or impulse conduction (block, re-entry). Arrhythmias are classified in several overlapping ways: by rate (bradyarrhythmia, under 60/min; tachyarrhythmia, over 100/min), by anatomical origin (supraventricular, arising above the AV node; junctional, arising from the AV node itself; ventricular, arising below the AV node), and by QRS duration (narrow complex, under 120 ms; wide complex, 120 ms or more).</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Atrial fibrillation alone affects more than 20% of the general population at some point in their life, and 2–3% of the UK population at any one time, rising steeply with age. AV nodal re-entrant tachycardia (AVNRT), the most common regular supraventricular tachycardia, has an estimated incidence of around 35 per 10,000 person-years. Most other arrhythmias are individually far less prevalent, though ventricular arrhythmias carry disproportionate clinical weight because of sudden death risk.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<ul><li><strong>Structural heart disease:</strong> ischaemia, cardiomyopathy, valve disease</li><li><strong>Electrolyte disturbance:</strong> potassium, magnesium, calcium</li><li><strong>Drugs:</strong> QT-prolonging agents, digoxin</li><li><strong>Thyroid disease</strong></li><li><strong>Inherited channelopathies:</strong> long QT syndrome, Brugada syndrome, an accessory pathway in WPW syndrome</li><li><strong>Autonomic influences:</strong> high vagal tone causing bradycardia in athletes</li></ul>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>Two broad mechanisms explain most tachyarrhythmias. <strong>Abnormal automaticity</strong> occurs when a focus depolarises spontaneously faster than the sinus node. <strong>Re-entry</strong> occurs when an anatomical or functional circuit allows a wave of depolarisation to keep circulating instead of extinguishing. This is the basis of AVNRT, AVRT via an accessory pathway in WPW syndrome, atrial flutter's macro-re-entrant circuit, and most ventricular tachycardia. Re-entry requires a region of slow conduction alongside unidirectional block, so the circulating wavefront never meets tissue that is still refractory from the beat before.</p><p>In AVNRT, the re-entry circuit sits entirely within the AV node itself, exploiting the node's two functionally distinct conduction pathways, a fast one and a slow one, to sustain the loop. In orthodromic AVRT, the more common pattern in WPW syndrome, the impulse travels forward (antegrade) through the AV node and back (retrograde) via the accessory pathway, producing a narrow QRS complex that can be difficult to distinguish from AVNRT on the surface ECG alone.</p><p>Conduction block instead arises when the specialised conducting tissue, the SA node, AV node, or His-Purkinje system, is diseased or pharmacologically suppressed, producing bradyarrhythmia at a severity proportional to the level blocked.</p><div class=\"callout\"><span class=\"callout-label\">Why this matters for the exam</span><p>Long QT syndrome, congenital or acquired, prolongs ventricular repolarisation, creating a vulnerable window in which an early afterdepolarisation can trigger torsades de pointes. This is why so many unrelated exam scenarios (electrolyte loss, a QT-prolonging antiemetic, a macrolide antibiotic) all converge on the same feared endpoint.</p></div>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p>Palpitations, presyncope or syncope, breathlessness and chest discomfort are the core presenting symptoms, classically of sudden onset with abrupt termination in re-entrant tachycardias. A meaningful proportion are asymptomatic and detected incidentally, particularly paroxysmal atrial fibrillation. Decompensated patients may show signs of heart failure on examination: bibasal crackles, a third heart sound, and a raised jugular venous pressure.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<ul><li><strong>12-lead ECG during symptoms:</strong> the single most useful test if it can be captured</li><li>Ambulatory monitoring (24–48h Holter, longer event recorder, or implantable loop recorder) if paroxysmal</li><li>Echocardiogram to assess for structural heart disease</li><li>Electrophysiological study where the mechanism needs defining, for example before ablation</li></ul>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Correct reversible causes first: electrolytes, ischaemia, thyroid disease. For narrow-complex re-entrant tachycardias such as AVNRT and orthodromic AVRT, vagal manoeuvres (Valsalva, or facial ice-water immersion) are first-line; if these fail, intravenous adenosine terminates the majority by producing a brief, complete conduction block at the AV node through A1 receptor activation. In atrial fibrillation, the choice between rate and rhythm control is separate from the anticoagulation decision, which is made by CHA<sub>2</sub>DS<sub>2</sub>-VASc regardless of which strategy is chosen. Catheter ablation is increasingly offered as first-line, rather than a fallback, for many re-entrant tachycardias given its high success rate. Bradyarrhythmia with haemodynamic compromise is treated with atropine, then pacing. Life-threatening ventricular arrhythmia is treated with defibrillation if pulseless, or synchronised cardioversion if a pulse is present with adverse signs; an ICD is considered for recurrent risk.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Thromboembolic stroke in atrial fibrillation, sudden cardiac death in ventricular arrhythmias and some channelopathies, tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy from prolonged uncontrolled rates, and heart failure from chronic bradycardia are the major long-term risks.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Narrow-complex versus broad-complex is the first branch point in any tachycardia question. Broad-complex is ventricular until proven otherwise.$t$, $t$In pre-excited atrial fibrillation (WPW syndrome), avoid AV-nodal blocking drugs: blocking the AV node can preferentially conduct impulses down the accessory pathway and precipitate ventricular fibrillation. Synchronised cardioversion, or ibutilide/procainamide, is preferred instead.$t$, $t$Shock for VF and pulseless VT. Use synchronised cardioversion for VT with a pulse and adverse signs.$t$],
  $j$[{"label":"Supraventricular tachycardia","source":"StatPearls (NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441972/"},{"label":"Arrhythmia classification and overview","source":"StatPearls (NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558923/"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Palpitations$t$, $t$Syncope and pre-syncope$t$, $t$Chest pain$t$],
  0
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Cardiac failure$t$,
  'cardiac-failure',
  $t$Why a syndrome defined by the heart “failing to pump enough” is, in practice, a story about compensation, and about what happens when the very systems that compensate for it start to cause harm.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Heart failure is a clinical syndrome rather than a single disease: the heart is unable to pump enough blood to meet the body's metabolic demands, or can only do so at abnormally elevated filling pressures. It is classified by ejection fraction, <strong>HFrEF</strong> (reduced, &lt;40%), <strong>HFmrEF</strong> (mildly reduced, 40–49%), and <strong>HFpEF</strong> (preserved, ≥50%), and by course, as chronic disease, an acute new presentation, or acute decompensation of chronic disease.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Affects roughly 1–2% of adults in the UK, rising to over 10% of those aged 70 and above, and is the leading cause of emergency hospital admission in older adults. Coronary heart disease and hypertension together account for the majority of cases.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<ul><li><strong>Ischaemic heart disease:</strong> the single most common cause</li><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> chronic pressure overload</li><li><strong>Valvular heart disease:</strong> chronic volume or pressure overload</li><li><strong>Cardiomyopathies:</strong> dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive</li><li><strong>Persistent arrhythmia:</strong> tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy</li><li><strong>Infective / toxic:</strong> myocarditis, alcohol excess, anthracycline or trastuzumab chemotherapy</li><li><strong>Infiltrative disease:</strong> amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, haemochromatosis</li><li><strong>High-output states:</strong> severe anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, arteriovenous fistula</li></ul>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>Almost every clinical feature and every drug class in heart failure follows from one idea: the compensations that rescue cardiac output in the short term are the same ones that cause harm if they persist.</p><p>An index insult, most often infarction, reduces stroke volume. Falling cardiac output is sensed as falling perfusion, and two neurohormonal systems switch on to defend it: the <strong>sympathetic nervous system</strong>, which raises heart rate and contractility, and the <strong>renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system</strong>, in which reduced renal perfusion triggers renin release, generating angiotensin II (vasoconstriction) and aldosterone (renal sodium and water retention, raising preload).</p><p>Acutely, this is adaptive. Sustained for weeks and months, it is not. Chronic RAAS and sympathetic drive push the ventricle toward pathological remodelling: myocyte hypertrophy, interstitial fibrosis, chamber dilation. Meanwhile the ongoing volume retention produces the congestive picture that actually brings patients to hospital: pulmonary oedema, a raised jugular venous pressure, peripheral oedema.</p><div class=\"callout\"><span class=\"callout-label\">Why this matters for the exam</span><p>The Frank–Starling relationship explains the U-turn in management. Initially, a higher filling pressure stretches the myocyte and increases contractile force. Past a critical point, though, the failing ventricle's Starling curve flattens and then descends: more volume no longer improves stroke volume, and can worsen it. That is the mechanistic reason a congested failing heart is treated with a diuretic, not a fluid bolus.</p></div><p>The ventricle itself pushes back against this cascade: stretched myocardium releases <strong>natriuretic peptides</strong> (BNP, NT-proBNP), which promote natriuresis and vasodilation, directly opposing RAAS. This is both a compensatory mechanism and the basis of the diagnostic blood test.</p><p>Seen this way, first-line drug therapy for HFrEF isn't a list to memorise so much as a direct rebuttal of this mechanism: ACE inhibitors and ARBs block the RAAS arm, beta-blockers blunt the sympathetic arm, and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists block aldosterone specifically.</p>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<table class=\"fact-table\"><thead><tr><th>Symptoms</th><th>Signs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Exertional breathlessness, progressing to orthopnoea</td><td>Raised jugular venous pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea</td><td>Bibasal crackles, third heart sound (S3)</td></tr><tr><td>Fatigue, reduced exercise tolerance</td><td>Peripheral pitting oedema</td></tr><tr><td>Ankle swelling</td><td>Hepatomegaly, ascites (right-sided / congestive)</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<ul><li><strong>NT-proBNP</strong> first-line: high negative predictive value, so a normal result makes heart failure unlikely</li><li><strong>Echocardiography</strong> to confirm the diagnosis and classify by ejection fraction</li><li><strong>ECG:</strong> rarely entirely normal in heart failure</li><li><strong>Chest X-ray:</strong> cardiomegaly, upper lobe diversion, Kerley B lines, pleural effusion, interstitial/alveolar oedema</li></ul>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p><strong>Acute decompensation:</strong> IV loop diuretic, oxygen if hypoxic, sit the patient upright, and identify and treat the precipitant.</p><p><strong>Chronic HFrEF:</strong> first line is an ACE inhibitor (or ARB) plus a beta-blocker, titrated slowly and not during an acute decompensation. Second line adds a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist. SGLT2 inhibitors are now foundational across the ejection-fraction spectrum. Specialist options, sacubitril-valsartan, ivabradine, CRT, ICD, are added against specific criteria.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death are leading causes of mortality in HFrEF. Progressive renal impairment (cardiorenal syndrome), thromboembolism, and cachexia in advanced disease are recognised complications. Prognosis at first hospital admission is often quoted as roughly 50% five-year mortality, comparable to many cancers.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$A normal NT-proBNP has high sensitivity. Don't anchor on heart failure as the diagnosis if it comes back normal.$t$, $t$Beta-blockers are for stable chronic disease, not acute decompensation. Starting one mid-episode can acutely worsen cardiac output.$t$, $t$The Frank–Starling curve is why the acute treatment is a diuretic, not a fluid bolus.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Breathlessness$t$, $t$Limb swelling$t$, $t$Palpitations$t$],
  1
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Cardiac involvement in genetic disease$t$,
  'genetic',
  $t$A single gene, one wrong protein, and a heart that looks structurally normal on the outside can still be the most dangerous organ in the room.$t$,
  $t$Inherited disease$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Inherited conditions in which a cardiac phenotype is a defining or major feature, spanning structural cardiomyopathies, ion channelopathies, and syndromic or connective tissue disease with cardiovascular involvement.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Individually rare (hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, the most common, still only affects around 1 in 500), but collectively an important cause of sudden cardiac death in the young, and a major driver of relative-screening referrals.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<ul><li><strong>Sarcomeric gene mutations:</strong> hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</li><li><strong>Ion channel mutations:</strong> long QT syndrome, Brugada syndrome</li><li><strong>Desmosomal mutations:</strong> arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy</li><li><strong>Fibrillin-1 mutation:</strong> Marfan syndrome, aortic root disease</li><li><strong>Chromosomal/syndromic disease:</strong> Turner syndrome (coarctation, bicuspid aortic valve), Down syndrome (atrioventricular septal defect)</li></ul>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>Sarcomeric mutations in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy cause myocyte disarray and hypertrophy, producing a stiff, non-compliant ventricle with diastolic dysfunction and, in obstructive forms, dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction from systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve. Disorganised myocardium is also inherently arrhythmogenic, which is the mechanistic link to sudden death in otherwise well young people.</p><p>Channelopathies alter the cardiac action potential directly, with no structural abnormality at all. Long QT syndrome prolongs repolarisation and predisposes to torsades; Brugada syndrome alters early repolarisation in the right ventricular outflow tract, producing a characteristic ECG pattern and a risk of polymorphic VT, typically at rest or with fever.</p><p>In Marfan syndrome, defective fibrillin-1 weakens the aortic media's elastic architecture, causing progressive aortic root dilatation and dissection risk: a structural, not electrical, consequence of the same broad category of genetic disease with cardiac involvement.</p>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p>Often asymptomatic until the first presentation is sudden cardiac death, particularly in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and the channelopathies. Where symptomatic: exertional syncope, palpitations, breathlessness. Syndromic features can point toward the diagnosis, such as Marfanoid habitus or Turner syndrome stigmata.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<ul><li>ECG, often abnormal before symptoms develop (LVH pattern in HCM, QT interval measurement, Brugada pattern)</li><li>Echocardiography and cardiac MRI for structural and fibrosis assessment</li><li>Genetic testing once an index case is identified, to allow cascade screening of relatives</li></ul>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Activity modification, particularly restriction from high-intensity competitive sport; beta-blockers; ICD implantation where risk stratification indicates significant sudden death risk; aortic root surveillance and prophylactic surgery in Marfan syndrome; and mandatory family cascade screening once a pathogenic variant is identified.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Sudden cardiac death is the defining risk across this group. Prognosis is highly gene- and mutation-specific, which is why genetic characterisation matters clinically and not just academically.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$A young, apparently fit person who collapses during exercise should prompt consideration of HCM or a channelopathy.$t$, $t$First-degree relatives of anyone diagnosed with an inherited cardiac condition need cascade screening, a management point tested in its own right.$t$, $t$Brugada-pattern VT classically occurs at rest or with fever, not on exertion: the opposite trigger pattern to HCM-related sudden death.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Syncope and pre-syncope$t$, $t$Palpitations$t$],
  2
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Cardiac involvement in infectious disease$t$,
  'infectious',
  $t$The heart can be directly infected, or caught in the crossfire of the immune response an infection elsewhere provokes, and the exam tests both.$t$,
  $t$Infection$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Covers direct cardiac infection (infective endocarditis, myocarditis, pericarditis) and cardiac involvement as part of a systemic infective or post-infective process, most classically rheumatic fever.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Infective endocarditis incidence has risen with prosthetic valve use, intravenous drug use and healthcare-associated bacteraemia. Rheumatic heart disease remains a leading global cause of valve disease, though now uncommon in the UK.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p><strong>Infective endocarditis:</strong> <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> is now the leading overall cause, particularly in IVDU and healthcare-associated disease; viridans streptococci from a dental source remain classic for native valve disease; enterococci are also recognised. <strong>Myocarditis:</strong> coxsackievirus and other enteroviruses are classic causes. <strong>Rheumatic fever:</strong> follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis.</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>Infective endocarditis begins with endothelial damage from turbulent flow across an abnormal or prosthetic valve, allowing platelet-fibrin deposition that becomes infected during transient bacteraemia. The resulting vegetation destroys the valve locally and showers septic emboli systemically, producing the classic peripheral stigmata.</p><div class=\"callout\"><span class=\"callout-label\">Why this matters for the exam</span><p>Rheumatic fever is not direct cardiac infection at all. It is molecular mimicry: antibodies raised against streptococcal M protein cross-react with cardiac myosin and valve tissue, causing an autoimmune pancarditis that, over repeated episodes, scars the mitral valve into stenosis. This is why long-term penicillin prophylaxis after a first episode is about preventing recurrent immune injury, not treating an ongoing infection.</p></div><p>Viral myocarditis causes direct cardiomyocyte injury plus a secondary T-cell-mediated immune response, which can produce acute pump failure or, if the immune response persists, a dilated cardiomyopathy.</p>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p><strong>Endocarditis:</strong> fever, a new or changing murmur, peripheral stigmata (splinter haemorrhages, Osler's nodes, Janeway lesions, Roth spots). <strong>Myocarditis:</strong> chest pain, breathlessness, arrhythmia, often following a viral prodrome. <strong>Rheumatic fever:</strong> migratory polyarthritis, carditis, subcutaneous nodules, erythema marginatum, Sydenham's chorea (the Jones criteria).</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Three sets of blood cultures before antibiotics, plus echocardiography, assessed against the modified Duke criteria for suspected endocarditis. Troponin and cardiac MRI for suspected myocarditis. Throat swab/ASOT titre and echocardiography for rheumatic fever.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Prolonged (typically 4–6 week) IV antibiotics for endocarditis, guided by organism and valve type, with surgery for heart failure, uncontrolled infection or high embolic risk. Supportive care for myocarditis. Aspirin/NSAIDs plus treatment of the streptococcal infection for rheumatic fever, with long-term penicillin prophylaxis to prevent recurrence.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Endocarditis carries significant in-hospital mortality even when treated. Repeated rheumatic fever episodes are a major cause of mitral stenosis. Fulminant myocarditis can progress rapidly to cardiogenic shock.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Routine antibiotic prophylaxis before dental procedures is no longer recommended by NICE, even with a prosthetic valve, a frequently tested change from older teaching.$t$, $t$New heart failure after a recent viral illness in a young patient should raise suspicion of myocarditis.$t$, $t$The Jones criteria diagnose rheumatic fever; the modified Duke criteria diagnose infective endocarditis. Don't mix them up.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Chest pain$t$, $t$Breathlessness$t$],
  3
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Congenital heart disease in the adult$t$,
  'congenital',
  $t$Repaired in childhood does not mean resolved in adulthood. This population now outnumbers the children it used to describe.$t$,
  $t$GUCH$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Structural cardiac defects present from birth, increasingly encountered in adulthood as paediatric survival has improved. \"Grown-up congenital heart disease\" (GUCH) now outnumbers children with congenital heart disease in developed healthcare systems.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Affects roughly 1% of live births. The lesions most often surviving to adulthood undiagnosed, or unrepaired but well tolerated, are atrial septal defect, bicuspid aortic valve, and mild pulmonary stenosis.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p>Mostly sporadic. Recognised associations include maternal rubella infection (patent ductus arteriosus, pulmonary stenosis), maternal diabetes, and chromosomal syndromes: Down syndrome (atrioventricular septal defect), Turner syndrome (coarctation, bicuspid aortic valve), 22q11 deletion (tetralogy of Fallot and other conotruncal defects).</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>The shared theme is abnormal shunting or obstruction established in fetal life. A septal defect allows blood to shunt along the pressure gradient, normally left-to-right, since systemic pressure exceeds pulmonary pressure, chronically volume-loading the pulmonary circulation.</p><div class=\"callout\"><span class=\"callout-label\">Why this matters for the exam</span><p>Over years, chronic volume overload can drive pulmonary vascular remodelling and rising pulmonary vascular resistance, until it exceeds systemic pressure and reverses the shunt direction: Eisenmenger syndrome. Deoxygenated blood now enters the systemic circulation and the patient becomes cyanosed. This is a late, largely irreversible complication, and closing the shunt at that stage can be fatal because the right ventricle has become dependent on the pop-off point the shunt provides.</p></div><p>Coarctation of the aorta produces proximal hypertension and reduced distal perfusion, with collateral vessels, visible radiologically as rib notching, developing to bypass the narrowing.</p>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p>Often asymptomatic for decades. May present with an incidental murmur, breathlessness, arrhythmia related to chronic chamber stretch, or cyanosis and clubbing once Eisenmenger physiology develops.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Echocardiography is first-line for most lesions. Cardiac MRI or CT for detailed anatomy, especially post-surgical. Cardiac catheterisation to measure pulmonary pressures and shunt fraction when reversibility is in question.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Many lesions are now closed percutaneously rather than surgically. Timing balances symptom burden against the risk of established pulmonary vascular disease, since repair after irreversible pulmonary hypertension has developed does not reverse it. Lifelong specialist follow-up is standard given the risk of arrhythmia and late complications even after successful repair.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Pulmonary hypertension and Eisenmenger syndrome, arrhythmia, endocarditis risk, and heart failure. Outcomes have improved dramatically with earlier diagnosis, but \"corrected\" is not equivalent to normal, and requires ongoing surveillance.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$A fixed, widely split second heart sound that doesn't vary with respiration is classic for ASD.$t$, $t$Once Eisenmenger physiology is established, closing the shunt can be fatal, a frequently tested exception to “close the defect.”$t$, $t$Rib notching on chest X-ray points to collateral vessels bypassing a coarctation.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Breathlessness$t$, $t$Palpitations$t$],
  4
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Coronary heart disease$t$,
  'coronary',
  $t$The same disease process, viewed at three different speeds: a fixed narrowing, a ruptured plaque, and a completely occluded artery.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Spans stable angina through the acute coronary syndromes (unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI) to chronic ischaemic cardiomyopathy, all arising from atherosclerotic narrowing or occlusion of the coronary arteries.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>The leading cause of death worldwide. UK prevalence has fallen substantially with risk factor modification and better acute treatment, but it remains extremely common.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p><strong>Modifiable:</strong> smoking, hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidaemia, obesity, sedentary lifestyle. <strong>Non-modifiable:</strong> age, male sex, family history, ethnicity (higher risk in South Asian populations).</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>Atherosclerosis begins with endothelial injury and lipid infiltration into the arterial intima, triggering an inflammatory response in which macrophages ingest oxidised LDL to become foam cells, forming a fatty streak that matures into a fibrous-capped plaque.</p><div class=\"callout\"><span class=\"callout-label\">Why this matters for the exam</span><p>Stable angina and acute coronary syndrome are the same disease at different mechanical stages. Stable angina occurs when a <em>fixed</em> plaque narrows the lumen enough that demand outstrips supply during exertion, producing predictable pain that's relieved by rest. Acute coronary syndrome occurs when a plaque's fibrous cap <em>ruptures or erodes</em>, exposing thrombogenic material and triggering platelet aggregation and thrombus. The completeness of the resulting occlusion determines whether it's unstable angina (no necrosis), NSTEMI (subendocardial infarction), or STEMI (transmural infarction from complete, persistent occlusion), which is exactly why stable disease is managed with anti-anginal drugs, while ACS is a medical emergency needing antiplatelet therapy and urgent reperfusion.</p></div>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p>Exertional central chest tightness radiating to the arm or jaw, relieved by rest or GTN (stable angina); the same pain at rest, more severe or prolonged, often with breathlessness, sweating and nausea (ACS). Presentation may be atypical or silent, particularly in diabetic or elderly patients.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Resting ECG, troponin and cardiac risk scoring for suspected ACS. CT coronary angiography is the NICE-preferred first-line investigation for stable chest pain of suspected cardiac origin, ahead of exercise ECG. Invasive coronary angiography where revascularisation is being considered.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p><strong>Stable disease:</strong> aspirin, statin, anti-anginal therapy (beta-blocker or calcium channel blocker first-line), risk factor modification, with revascularisation considered for refractory symptoms or high-risk anatomy. <strong>ACS:</strong> dual antiplatelet therapy, anticoagulation, and urgent reperfusion, primary PCI within the guideline window, or fibrinolysis where PCI cannot be delivered in time, for STEMI; a risk-stratified early invasive strategy for NSTEMI/unstable angina.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Arrhythmia including sudden cardiac death, heart failure, mechanical complications of infarction (papillary muscle rupture, ventricular septal rupture, free wall rupture), and recurrent ischaemic events. Prognosis has improved substantially with primary PCI but remains strongly linked to how quickly reperfusion is achieved.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$“Time is muscle”: the interval from symptom onset to reperfusion is the single biggest modifiable determinant of outcome in STEMI.$t$, $t$A normal troponin taken too early after symptom onset does not exclude MI and needs repeating per protocol.$t$, $t$CT coronary angiography, not exercise ECG, is now NICE's first-line test for stable chest pain of suspected cardiac origin.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Chest pain$t$, $t$Breathlessness$t$],
  5
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Diseases of heart muscle$t$,
  'heart-muscle',
  $t$Four cardiomyopathies, four different physics problems. Two of them, restrictive cardiomyopathy and constrictive pericarditis, form the highest-yield look-alike pair in the specialty.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>The cardiomyopathies: diseases of heart muscle not explained by coronary, valvular or hypertensive disease alone, classified structurally as dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive, and arrhythmogenic.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Dilated cardiomyopathy is the most common form and a leading indication for heart transplantation. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy affects around 1 in 500 people, most of whom remain undiagnosed.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p><strong>Dilated:</strong> idiopathic/genetic (up to a third of cases), alcohol, viral myocarditis, peripartum, anthracycline chemotherapy. <strong>Hypertrophic:</strong> sarcomeric gene mutations, usually autosomal dominant. <strong>Restrictive:</strong> infiltrative disease, amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, haemochromatosis. <strong>Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy:</strong> desmosomal gene mutations.</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>Each structural pattern produces a distinct physiological problem. Dilated cardiomyopathy features a thin-walled, poorly contractile, enlarged ventricle: a systolic dysfunction problem, converging on the same neurohormonal cascade as cardiac failure generally.</p><p>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy produces a thick, stiff, hypercontractile but poorly relaxing ventricle: a diastolic dysfunction problem, in which disorganised myocyte architecture additionally predisposes to fatal arrhythmia, and the hypertrophied septum can cause dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction.</p><div class=\"callout\"><span class=\"callout-label\">Why this matters for the exam</span><p>That obstruction is <em>dynamic</em>, not fixed. It worsens with manoeuvres that reduce ventricular volume (standing, Valsalva) and improves with those that increase it (squatting), the opposite behaviour to the fixed murmur of aortic stenosis, and a classic discriminator between the two.</p></div><p>Restrictive cardiomyopathy involves infiltration of the myocardium with abnormal material, producing a stiff ventricle with impaired filling but relatively preserved systolic function until late. Clinically it can closely mimic constrictive pericarditis, which is not a myocardial disease at all, but a pericardial one, making the distinction between the two a recurrent exam theme, since one is medical and the other surgically curable.</p>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p>Breathlessness and fatigue (dilated); exertional dyspnoea, syncope, sudden death risk, and a murmur that increases with Valsalva (hypertrophic); breathlessness and oedema with a relatively preserved ejection fraction (restrictive).</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Echocardiography to define the structural pattern; cardiac MRI for detailed tissue characterisation, particularly to identify infiltrative disease and quantify fibrosis; genetic testing and family screening once a heritable cause is identified; endomyocardial biopsy occasionally for restrictive disease.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Standard heart failure therapy for dilated cardiomyopathy; beta-blockers, activity modification and ICD risk stratification for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; treatment directed at the underlying infiltrative cause where identifiable for restrictive disease, which otherwise responds poorly to conventional heart failure therapy.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Sudden cardiac death across all subtypes, particularly hypertrophic and arrhythmogenic forms; progressive heart failure; thromboembolism from a dilated, poorly contractile chamber.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$A murmur that gets louder on standing/Valsalva and quieter on squatting points to HOCM, the opposite pattern to aortic stenosis.$t$, $t$Restrictive cardiomyopathy and constrictive pericarditis can look identical clinically but need different treatment. Imaging-based distinction is the key management step.$t$, $t$ARVC classically presents with ventricular arrhythmia in a young person, often triggered by exercise.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Breathlessness$t$, $t$Syncope and pre-syncope$t$],
  6
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Diseases of the arteries, including aortic dissection$t$,
  'arteries',
  $t$Laplace's law, a torn intima, and a plaque that runs out of oxygen: three different mechanical failures of the same vessel wall.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Covers aneurysmal, dissecting, occlusive and inflammatory disease of the aorta and peripheral arteries.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Abdominal aortic aneurysm affects around 1 in 25 men over 65, hence the UK national screening programme. Aortic dissection is rarer but rapidly fatal if missed.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p>Atherosclerosis and hypertension (most peripheral arterial disease and many aneurysms), connective tissue disease (Marfan syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome: dissection at a younger age), bicuspid aortic valve (aortic root disease), vasculitis (giant cell arteritis, Takayasu arteritis), and smoking, the strongest modifiable risk factor for both aneurysm and peripheral arterial disease.</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>Aneurysmal disease reflects progressive degeneration of the arterial wall's elastic and muscular layers. Dilation follows Laplace's law: as radius increases, wall tension rises for a given pressure, accelerating further dilation and raising rupture risk disproportionately once a critical diameter is passed.</p><p>Aortic dissection begins with a tear in the intima, allowing pressurised blood to enter and split the media, creating a false lumen that propagates along the vessel. This can occlude branch vessels as it extends, explaining the variable presentation of stroke, limb ischaemia, or myocardial infarction from coronary ostial involvement, depending on which branches are compromised, and can rupture through the adventitia, which is rapidly fatal.</p><p>Peripheral arterial disease follows the same atherosclerotic process as coronary disease, producing exertional limb ischaemia (claudication) when demand outstrips a stenosed vessel's supply, progressing to critical limb ischaemia with rest pain and tissue loss as disease advances.</p>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p><strong>Aneurysm:</strong> usually asymptomatic until rupture (sudden severe abdominal/back pain, hypotension) or found on screening. <strong>Dissection:</strong> sudden, severe, tearing chest or back pain, maximal at onset, with a blood pressure or pulse discrepancy between limbs. <strong>Peripheral arterial disease:</strong> intermittent claudication, progressing to rest pain and ulceration.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Ultrasound for aneurysm screening and surveillance. CT angiography is the definitive investigation for suspected dissection, and is also used for aneurysm sizing prior to intervention. Ankle-brachial pressure index and duplex ultrasound for peripheral arterial disease.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Elective aneurysm repair above a size threshold or with rapid growth. Type A dissection (involving the ascending aorta) is a surgical emergency; type B (descending aorta only) is usually managed medically first, with strict blood pressure control, unless complicated by malperfusion. Peripheral arterial disease: risk factor modification, antiplatelet therapy, statin, supervised exercise, with revascularisation for critical limb ischaemia.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Rupture (aneurysm), malperfusion syndromes and death (dissection), critical limb ischaemia and amputation (peripheral arterial disease). Mortality from untreated type A dissection rises steeply with every hour of delay.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Tearing pain radiating to the back with unequal arm blood pressures should trigger urgent CT angiography before assuming a cardiac or musculoskeletal cause.$t$, $t$Stanford type A involves the ascending aorta and is surgical; type B does not, and is usually managed medically. A near-guaranteed exam distinction.$t$, $t$Smoking cessation is the single most effective intervention in peripheral arterial disease.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Chest pain$t$, $t$Limb pain$t$],
  7
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Diseases of the pulmonary circulation$t$,
  'pulmonary-circulation',
  $t$When the right ventricle is the one under attack: either suddenly, from a clot, or slowly, from years of rising resistance.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Covers pulmonary hypertension of any cause and pulmonary embolism, the two major ways the pulmonary vasculature itself becomes the primary problem, rather than a secondary bystander to left heart or lung disease.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Pulmonary embolism is common and frequently under-recognised. Idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension is rare, but pulmonary hypertension secondary to other conditions (left heart disease, chronic lung disease, chronic thromboembolic disease) is much more frequent.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p><strong>PE:</strong> usually embolisation from a lower limb DVT, with risk factors following Virchow's triad: immobility, malignancy, surgery, the combined oral contraceptive pill, pregnancy, thrombophilia. <strong>Pulmonary hypertension:</strong> classified into five groups by mechanism, including idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension, connective tissue disease (particularly systemic sclerosis), left heart disease, chronic lung disease/hypoxia, and chronic thromboembolic disease.</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>An embolus lodging in the pulmonary arterial tree causes an acute rise in pulmonary vascular resistance and right ventricular afterload. A large enough embolus, or a smaller one in a patient with limited cardiopulmonary reserve, can precipitate acute right ventricular strain and failure, with the interventricular septum bulging into the left ventricle and compromising left-sided filling and cardiac output: the mechanism behind haemodynamically significant (\"massive\") PE.</p><p>Chronic pulmonary hypertension, whatever its trigger, converges on a shared final pathway: sustained elevated pressure drives progressive remodelling of the small pulmonary arteries (medial hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis), which is itself self-perpetuating and further raises resistance, ultimately causing right ventricular hypertrophy and, if unchecked, right heart failure (cor pulmonale).</p>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p><strong>PE:</strong> acute breathlessness, pleuritic chest pain, haemoptysis, tachycardia, and in massive PE, syncope and shock. <strong>Pulmonary hypertension:</strong> progressive exertional breathlessness, with signs of right heart strain (raised JVP, loud P2, peripheral oedema) as it advances.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Wells score to stratify pre-test probability of PE, guiding whether D-dimer or direct CT pulmonary angiography is appropriate. Echocardiography is the first-line non-invasive test for suspected pulmonary hypertension, with right heart catheterisation required to confirm the diagnosis and measure pressures directly.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Therapeutic anticoagulation for PE, with thrombolysis reserved for haemodynamically unstable (\"massive\") PE. Pulmonary hypertension management targets the underlying group/cause where possible; pulmonary vasodilator therapy is reserved specifically for pulmonary arterial hypertension, since using it in pulmonary hypertension secondary to left heart or lung disease can worsen outcomes.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension can develop after incompletely resolved PE. Right heart failure is the eventual common endpoint of unchecked pulmonary hypertension of any cause.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$D-dimer only helps rule out PE in low pre-test probability patients. In a high-probability patient, go straight to CT pulmonary angiography.$t$, $t$A loud, palpable P2 is a key bedside clue to pulmonary hypertension.$t$, $t$Pulmonary vasodilators can worsen outcomes if used in the wrong group of pulmonary hypertension. Correct classification matters before treating.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Breathlessness$t$, $t$Chest pain$t$],
  8
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Heart valve disease$t$,
  'valve-disease',
  $t$Pressure overload builds a thicker wall. Volume overload builds a bigger room. Almost everything else in valve disease follows from that one distinction.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Stenotic lesions (a valve that opens inadequately, obstructing forward flow) and regurgitant lesions (a valve that closes inadequately, allowing backward flow), affecting any of the four valves but most often the mitral and aortic valves.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Calcific aortic stenosis has become the most common valve lesion in the developed world as populations age. Rheumatic mitral stenosis remains common where childhood streptococcal treatment access is limited.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p><strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> age-related calcific degeneration; bicuspid aortic valve, presenting decades earlier. <strong>Mitral stenosis:</strong> almost always rheumatic. <strong>Aortic/mitral regurgitation:</strong> myxomatous degeneration (mitral valve prolapse), infective endocarditis, aortic root dilation, connective tissue disease, and as a downstream consequence of untreated hypertension or ischaemic papillary muscle dysfunction.</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>A stenotic valve forces the upstream chamber to generate higher pressure to maintain forward flow across a fixed, narrowed orifice. In aortic stenosis this produces concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, an adaptive response to chronic pressure overload that itself impairs diastolic relaxation and coronary perfusion reserve, which is why severe aortic stenosis can cause angina even without coexisting coronary disease.</p><div class=\"callout\"><span class=\"callout-label\">Why this matters for the exam</span><p>A regurgitant valve instead produces chronic <em>volume</em> overload of the receiving chamber. In aortic regurgitation the left ventricle dilates to accommodate the regurgitant volume, compensating via the Frank-Starling mechanism before eventually decompensating into systolic dysfunction if not corrected in time. Pressure overload (concentric hypertrophy) versus volume overload (eccentric dilation) is the single distinction underlying why the timing and nature of intervention differs between stenotic and regurgitant lesions.</p></div>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p>Exertional breathlessness, syncope and angina form the classic triad of severe aortic stenosis. Breathlessness and atrial fibrillation from left atrial enlargement occur in mitral stenosis. Each lesion has a characteristic murmur: ejection systolic radiating to the carotids (aortic stenosis), pansystolic radiating to the axilla (mitral regurgitation), early diastolic (aortic regurgitation), mid-diastolic with an opening snap (mitral stenosis).</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Echocardiography is the key investigation for every valve lesion, defining severity, ventricular function and the need for/timing of intervention. Exercise testing can unmask symptoms in apparently asymptomatic severe stenosis.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Intervention, surgical or transcatheter valve replacement/repair, is guided by symptom onset and objective markers of ventricular decompensation rather than valve severity alone in many cases: operating too early exposes unnecessary procedural risk, operating too late risks irreversible ventricular damage. Medical therapy has limited role in stenotic lesions, since no drug relieves a fixed mechanical obstruction, but is more relevant in regurgitant lesions and in bridging patients to intervention.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Once severe aortic stenosis becomes symptomatic, average survival without intervention is measured in a small number of years. Atrial fibrillation and thromboembolism are common in mitral valve disease. Infective endocarditis risk is heightened on any abnormal or prosthetic valve.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$The onset of symptoms, not the echocardiographic gradient alone, is the trigger for intervention in aortic stenosis.$t$, $t$A slow-rising carotid pulse (pulsus parvus et tardus) is a classic bedside sign of severe aortic stenosis.$t$, $t$Pressure overload builds thickness; volume overload builds size. Use that to predict which lesion produces which ventricular pattern.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Breathlessness$t$, $t$Syncope and pre-syncope$t$, $t$Chest pain$t$],
  9
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Hypertension$t$,
  'hypertension',
  $t$The kidney, not just the artery, sets the thermostat, which is why so much of hypertension management is really about sodium handling.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Sustained elevation of arterial blood pressure above threshold values that confer increased cardiovascular risk: the single most important modifiable risk factor for stroke, coronary disease and chronic kidney disease worldwide.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Affects around a third of UK adults, with prevalence rising steeply with age; a large proportion remains undiagnosed or inadequately controlled.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p><strong>Primary/essential</strong> hypertension has no single identifiable cause and accounts for the great majority of cases, driven by a combination of genetic predisposition, diet, obesity and lifestyle. <strong>Secondary</strong> causes, renal artery stenosis, primary hyperaldosteronism, phaeochromocytoma, Cushing's syndrome, coarctation of the aorta, and drugs such as NSAIDs, the combined oral contraceptive pill and corticosteroids, should be considered in young patients, resistant hypertension, or hypertension with suggestive additional features.</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>Blood pressure is the product of cardiac output and systemic vascular resistance, but essential hypertension is now understood as a disorder of renal sodium handling as much as of the vasculature itself. The kidney's pressure-natriuresis relationship, the tendency to excrete more sodium as pressure rises, is reset upward, so a higher arterial pressure is required to achieve sodium balance, perpetuating the elevated pressure.</p><p>Chronically elevated pressure imposes a pressure load on the left ventricle, driving concentric hypertrophy analogous to that seen in aortic stenosis, and accelerates atherosclerosis throughout the arterial tree by increasing endothelial shear stress, linking hypertension mechanistically to coronary disease, stroke and peripheral arterial disease. In the kidney, sustained pressure damages the glomerular microvasculature (hypertensive nephropathy); in the retina it produces the staged retinopathy changes used clinically to gauge chronicity and severity.</p>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p>Usually asymptomatic, \"the silent killer\", and detected on routine measurement. Severe/malignant hypertension can present with headache, visual disturbance, and features of end-organ damage: papilloedema, acute kidney injury, encephalopathy.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Confirmation with ambulatory or home blood pressure monitoring, to exclude white-coat hypertension, before diagnosis. Assessment for end-organ damage and cardiovascular risk: urinalysis, renal function, HbA1c, lipids, ECG, fundoscopy. Further work-up for secondary causes where clinically indicated.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>NICE's staged approach: ACE inhibitor/ARB first-line under 55 or with type 2 diabetes; calcium channel blocker first-line over 55 or of Black African/African-Caribbean origin, in whom ACE inhibitors are less effective as monotherapy. Stepwise addition of a thiazide-like diuretic. Resistant hypertension (uncontrolled on three drugs) prompts a check for postural hypotension, adherence and secondary causes before adding a fourth agent, guided by potassium level: spironolactone if low-normal, an alpha- or beta-blocker if high.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Stroke, coronary artery disease, heart failure, atrial fibrillation, aortic aneurysm/dissection, chronic kidney disease, and hypertensive retinopathy. Risk reduction from treatment is well established and roughly proportional to the degree of blood pressure lowering achieved.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Never diagnose hypertension from a single clinic reading. Ambulatory or home monitoring is required first.$t$, $t$A young patient with hypertension and hypokalaemia should prompt consideration of primary hyperaldosteronism.$t$, $t$ACE inhibitors are less effective as monotherapy in Black African/African-Caribbean patients. Calcium channel blockers are first-line instead.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Breathlessness$t$],
  10
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Hyperlipidaemia$t$,
  'hyperlipidaemia',
  $t$Statins don't strengthen the heart. They exploit the exact same receptor pathway that's congenitally broken in familial hypercholesterolaemia.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Elevated circulating lipid levels, principally LDL cholesterol, but also triglycerides and, protectively, HDL cholesterol, that drive atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Extremely common in Western populations, closely tied to diet, obesity and physical inactivity, though a genetic component, familial hypercholesterolaemia, affecting around 1 in 250 people, causes markedly elevated LDL from birth independent of lifestyle.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p>Polygenic/lifestyle-driven hyperlipidaemia (diet, obesity, sedentary behaviour, alcohol) is by far the most common pattern. Familial hypercholesterolaemia is autosomal dominant, most often due to LDL receptor mutations impairing hepatic LDL clearance. Secondary causes include hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, diabetes, cholestatic liver disease and certain drugs.</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>LDL particles that persist at high concentration in plasma are more likely to enter the arterial intima, where oxidative modification renders them pro-inflammatory and triggers the same atherosclerotic cascade described under coronary heart disease. Hyperlipidaemia is not a separate disease process from atherosclerosis so much as its principal upstream driver, which is why lowering LDL reduces cardiovascular events even in patients who already have established plaque.</p><div class=\"callout\"><span class=\"callout-label\">Why this matters for the exam</span><p>In familial hypercholesterolaemia, a non-functional LDL receptor means hepatocytes cannot clear circulating LDL efficiently, producing decades of additional cumulative arterial exposure and premature coronary disease, often in the third or fourth decade of life. Statins competitively inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, lowering intracellular cholesterol synthesis; the resulting fall <em>upregulates</em> hepatic LDL receptor expression, the same receptor pathway that's congenitally deficient in familial hypercholesterolaemia. It's cumulative exposure over time, not just peak level, that drives risk.</p></div>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p>Usually asymptomatic and detected on screening. Familial hypercholesterolaemia may present with tendon xanthomata (particularly Achilles), xanthelasma, corneal arcus before age 45, or a strong family history of premature coronary disease.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Fasting or non-fasting lipid profile. Clinical scoring criteria (e.g. Dutch Lipid Clinic Network Criteria), supported by genetic testing, where familial hypercholesterolaemia is suspected. Cardiovascular risk scoring (e.g. QRISK) to guide treatment thresholds in primary prevention.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Lifestyle modification for all patients. Statin therapy for primary prevention above a calculated risk threshold, and for all patients with established cardiovascular disease. Ezetimibe or PCSK9 inhibitors added where LDL targets aren't met on statin alone, particularly relevant in familial hypercholesterolaemia. Cascade testing of relatives once an index case of familial hypercholesterolaemia is identified.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Premature atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. Untreated familial hypercholesterolaemia carries a very substantially elevated lifetime risk of early coronary events.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Tendon xanthomata plus a family history of early MI should prompt consideration of familial hypercholesterolaemia, not routine polygenic hyperlipidaemia.$t$, $t$Statins are contraindicated in pregnancy.$t$, $t$Statins work by upregulating hepatic LDL receptors, the exact pathway congenitally deficient in FH.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Chest pain$t$],
  11
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Oedema$t$,
  'oedema',
  $t$A sign, not a diagnosis, and the Starling equation is the single framework that explains every one of its causes.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Excess fluid accumulation in the interstitial space, most often assessed in the lower limbs but relevant wherever it occurs (pulmonary, sacral, generalised/anasarca). A sign with cardiac, renal, hepatic, venous and lymphatic causes, not a diagnosis in itself.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Extremely common, especially peripheral oedema in older adults, where it is frequently multifactorial: a combination of venous insufficiency, immobility, and calcium channel blocker use, for example, rather than a single cause.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<ul><li><strong>Cardiac:</strong> right-sided or biventricular heart failure</li><li><strong>Renal:</strong> nephrotic syndrome, fluid overload in kidney disease</li><li><strong>Hepatic:</strong> cirrhosis, with hypoalbuminaemia and portal hypertension</li><li><strong>Venous:</strong> chronic venous insufficiency, deep vein thrombosis</li><li><strong>Lymphatic:</strong> lymphoedema, from obstruction or absence of drainage</li><li><strong>Drug-induced:</strong> calcium channel blockers, particularly dihydropyridines</li></ul>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>Fluid movement across the capillary wall is governed by Starling forces: the balance between capillary hydrostatic pressure (favouring filtration out of the vessel) and plasma oncotic pressure (favouring reabsorption), opposed by the equivalent interstitial pressures.</p><div class=\"callout\"><span class=\"callout-label\">Why this matters for the exam</span><p>Every cause of oedema is a disruption of one term in that same equation: raised capillary hydrostatic pressure (venous congestion in heart failure or venous insufficiency), reduced plasma oncotic pressure (hypoalbuminaemia in nephrotic syndrome or cirrhosis), increased capillary permeability (inflammation), or impaired lymphatic drainage (lymphoedema, where the overflow pathway itself is obstructed). Cardiac and renal oedema is further amplified by the same RAAS-driven sodium and water retention seen in heart failure, since a falling effective circulating volume, whether from reduced cardiac output or fluid shifting out of the vasculature, is sensed by the kidney as hypovolaemia, triggering more retention that paradoxically worsens the oedema.</p></div>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p>Distribution and character help localise the cause: pitting, dependent, bilateral oedema with a raised JVP suggests cardiac disease; pitting oedema with proteinuria and a normal JVP suggests a low-oncotic-pressure (renal/hepatic) cause; unilateral oedema suggests a local venous or lymphatic cause and should prompt consideration of DVT; non-pitting oedema suggests lymphoedema or myxoedema.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Directed by the clinical picture: NT-proBNP and echocardiogram if cardiac cause suspected; urinalysis, albumin and renal function if renal; liver function and ultrasound if hepatic; venous Doppler if unilateral or DVT is a concern.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Treat the underlying cause where possible. Diuretics for fluid overload from cardiac, renal or hepatic causes, used cautiously in cirrhotic ascites given the risk of precipitating hepatorenal syndrome or encephalopathy. Compression stockings and leg elevation for venous causes. Specific lymphoedema management (compression, manual lymphatic drainage), since diuretics are not effective there.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Dependent on the underlying cause. Chronic venous oedema can progress to venous ulceration and skin changes (lipodermatosclerosis). Recurrent cellulitis is a recognised complication of chronic lymphoedema.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Unilateral leg swelling is a DVT until proven otherwise, not “just oedema.”$t$, $t$Diuretics do not work for lymphoedema. The problem is failure of drainage, not excess fluid production.$t$, $t$Every cause of oedema maps to one broken term in the Starling equation. Use that to organise the differential.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Limb swelling$t$],
  12
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Pericardial disease$t$,
  'pericardial',
  $t$It's not the volume of fluid around the heart that kills. It's how fast that volume arrived.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Disorders of the fibrous sac surrounding the heart: acute pericarditis (inflammation), pericardial effusion (fluid accumulation, which can progress to tamponade), and constrictive pericarditis (chronic scarring and rigidity).</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Acute pericarditis is relatively common and often idiopathic/viral in the UK. Tamponade and constriction are less common but important not to miss, given their high morbidity/mortality if unrecognised.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p>Viral infection (the most common identified cause of acute pericarditis), post-myocardial infarction (early peri-infarction pericarditis, or the later autoimmune Dressler's syndrome), uraemia, malignancy, tuberculosis (an important cause of constrictive pericarditis worldwide), autoimmune disease, and iatrogenic causes (post-cardiac surgery, radiotherapy).</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>Acute pericarditis causes inflammation and roughening of the pericardial layers, producing the friction rub heard on auscultation and diffuse ST elevation on ECG, from subepicardial inflammation, distinct from the territorial ST elevation of infarction.</p><div class=\"callout\"><span class=\"callout-label\">Why this matters for the exam</span><p>A pericardial effusion becomes dangerous not because of its volume, but because of the <em>rate</em> of accumulation relative to pericardial compliance. The normal pericardium can stretch to accommodate a slowly accumulating large effusion with little haemodynamic effect, whereas even a small volume of blood accumulating rapidly, after trauma, or an aortic dissection rupturing into the pericardial space, can raise intrapericardial pressure faster than the sac can stretch, producing tamponade: rising pressure compresses all four chambers, most critically impairing diastolic filling, producing pulsus paradoxus as the ventricles compete for a fixed total pericardial volume with each breath.</p></div><p>Constrictive pericarditis instead reflects a chronically thickened, fibrotic, sometimes calcified pericardium that limits diastolic filling once the ventricles reach the pericardium's fixed volume early in diastole, a physiological picture that can closely resemble restrictive cardiomyopathy despite an entirely different, and surgically treatable, underlying problem.</p>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p><strong>Acute pericarditis:</strong> sharp, pleuritic chest pain, worse lying flat, relieved by sitting forward, with a friction rub. <strong>Tamponade:</strong> Beck's triad, hypotension, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds, tachycardia, pulsus paradoxus. <strong>Constriction:</strong> right heart failure signs with a preserved ejection fraction, and Kussmaul's sign, JVP rising, rather than falling, with inspiration.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>ECG and echocardiography for acute pericarditis/effusion. Echocardiography is the key initial test for suspected tamponade, though the diagnosis is ultimately clinical and shouldn't be delayed for imaging in an unstable patient. CT or MRI to demonstrate pericardial thickening/calcification in suspected constriction, with cardiac catheterisation to confirm the haemodynamic pattern.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>NSAIDs and colchicine for acute pericarditis, colchicine specifically reduces recurrence risk. Urgent pericardiocentesis for tamponade. Pericardiectomy for symptomatic constrictive pericarditis, since the fibrotic pericardium does not respond to medical therapy.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Recurrent pericarditis affects a meaningful minority after a first episode. Untreated tamponade is rapidly fatal. Constrictive pericarditis, once symptomatic, generally requires surgery for durable improvement.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Tamponade is a clinical diagnosis requiring immediate treatment. Don't wait for echocardiography in an unstable patient.$t$, $t$Distinguishing constrictive pericarditis from restrictive cardiomyopathy matters enormously: one is surgically curable, the other isn't.$t$, $t$Diffuse (not territorial) ST elevation with a friction rub points to pericarditis, not infarction.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Chest pain$t$, $t$Breathlessness$t$],
  13
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Tumours of the heart$t$,
  'tumours',
  $t$Rare, and a notorious mimic. A myxoma can copy the fever of endocarditis, the murmur of mitral stenosis, and the embolic strokes of both at once.$t$,
  $t$Uncommon$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Comprises primary cardiac tumours, overwhelmingly benign, with myxoma the most common in adults, and, far more common in absolute terms, secondary metastatic deposits from cancer elsewhere.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>Primary cardiac tumours are rare, with an estimated prevalence far below 1 in 1000. Metastatic cardiac involvement is more common than any primary cardiac tumour, reflecting the much higher overall incidence of malignancy generally.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p>Cardiac myxoma is usually sporadic, arising most often from the interatrial septum in the left atrium, though a minority occur as part of Carney complex, an inherited syndrome. Secondary cardiac tumours reflect direct extension or haematogenous/lymphatic spread from a primary malignancy, most often lung and breast cancer, melanoma, and lymphoma.</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>A myxoma's clinical impact comes from its physical presence rather than any local invasiveness. A pedunculated, gelatinous mass attached to the atrial septum can prolapse through the mitral valve during diastole, intermittently obstructing left ventricular filling in a manner that mimics mitral stenosis, with symptoms classically varying with posture as the tumour shifts under gravity.</p><div class=\"callout\"><span class=\"callout-label\">Why this matters for the exam</span><p>The friable surface of a myxoma is prone to fragmenting, so systemic embolisation of tumour material is common and can mimic the embolic phenomena of infective endocarditis. The tumour can also provoke a systemic inflammatory response, fever, weight loss, raised inflammatory markers, further blurring the picture with endocarditis or vasculitis. This is exactly why a patient being worked up for suspected endocarditis, with negative blood cultures, should have myxoma on the differential.</p></div>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p>Constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss, malaise) mimicking chronic infection or malignancy; positional breathlessness or syncope from intermittent valve obstruction; embolic events from tumour fragmentation; occasionally an audible \"tumour plop,\" an early diastolic sound from the mass striking the endocardium.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Echocardiography usually first identifies a cardiac mass. Cardiac MRI for detailed tissue characterisation and to help distinguish tumour from thrombus or vegetation. Histology after surgical excision confirms the diagnosis.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Surgical excision is the definitive treatment for myxoma and is usually curative, though a small recurrence risk exists, particularly in familial (Carney complex) cases. Management of secondary cardiac involvement is directed at the underlying malignancy, with pericardiocentesis or a pericardial window for symptomatic malignant effusion.</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Excellent prognosis after complete myxoma excision. Secondary cardiac involvement generally reflects advanced malignancy and carries the prognosis of the underlying cancer.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Constitutional symptoms with negative blood cultures in suspected endocarditis should prompt consideration of atrial myxoma.$t$, $t$Symptoms that vary with position are a classic, if not always present, clue to a mobile intracardiac mass.$t$, $t$Metastatic cardiac involvement is more common overall than any primary cardiac tumour.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Breathlessness$t$, $t$Syncope and pre-syncope$t$],
  14
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;

insert into chapters (exam, topic_id, status, title, slug, dek, tag, sections, pearls, refs, related_presentations, sort_order) values (
  'mrcp1',
  '30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
  'published',
  $t$Venous thromboembolism$t$,
  'vte',
  $t$One disease, two addresses. Most pulmonary emboli begin their journey as a deep vein thrombosis that simply moved.$t$,
  $t$Core exam topic$t$,
  $j$[{"heading":"Overview","body_html":"<p>Covers deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and its major complication, pulmonary embolism (PE), together representing a single disease process at two anatomical sites, since most PE originates as embolised DVT.</p>"},{"heading":"Epidemiology","body_html":"<p>A common and significant cause of preventable in-hospital mortality. Risk rises substantially with hospitalisation, surgery, malignancy, pregnancy and increasing age.</p>"},{"heading":"Aetiology","body_html":"<p>Virchow's triad frames the risk factors: <strong>venous stasis</strong> (immobility, long-haul travel, hospitalisation), <strong>hypercoagulability</strong> (malignancy, pregnancy, the combined oral contraceptive pill/HRT, inherited thrombophilia such as factor V Leiden), and <strong>endothelial injury</strong> (surgery, trauma, central venous catheters).</p>"},{"heading":"Pathophysiology","body_html":"<p>Venous thrombosis begins where blood flow is slowest and most turbulent, classically in the valve pockets of the deep leg veins, where stasis allows activated clotting factors to accumulate locally rather than being washed away and diluted by flowing blood. Endothelial injury or an underlying hypercoagulable state further tips the local balance from the normally anticoagulant, anti-adhesive endothelial surface toward a pro-thrombotic one.</p><p>Once a thrombus forms it can propagate proximally, and a fragment, or the whole clot, can dislodge and travel via the inferior vena cava and right heart to lodge in the pulmonary arterial tree as a PE, where its haemodynamic impact follows directly from pulmonary circulation physiology: an acute rise in right ventricular afterload, proportional to the size and location of the embolus.</p>"},{"heading":"Clinical features","body_html":"<p><strong>DVT:</strong> unilateral leg swelling, pain, warmth, tenderness along the deep venous system. <strong>PE:</strong> acute breathlessness, pleuritic chest pain, haemoptysis, tachycardia, and in large PE, syncope or shock.</p>"},{"heading":"Investigations","body_html":"<p>Wells score for DVT, and separately for PE, to stratify pre-test probability. D-dimer to help exclude the diagnosis only in low-probability patients: a raised D-dimer is non-specific and does not confirm VTE. Compression ultrasound for suspected DVT. CT pulmonary angiography for suspected PE with a high pre-test probability, or a positive D-dimer in a low-probability patient.</p>"},{"heading":"Management","body_html":"<p>Direct oral anticoagulants are now first-line for most patients with confirmed DVT or PE, in preference to warfarin, except in specific situations, antiphospholipid syndrome, severe renal impairment, pregnancy, where alternative anticoagulation is preferred. Thrombolysis is reserved for haemodynamically unstable (\"massive\") PE. Duration of anticoagulation depends on whether the event was provoked by a reversible risk factor (typically 3 months) or unprovoked/recurrent (often extended, balanced against bleeding risk).</p>"},{"heading":"Complications & prognosis","body_html":"<p>Post-thrombotic syndrome after DVT, chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension after PE, and recurrence risk, highest in unprovoked events and ongoing hypercoagulable states such as active malignancy.</p>"}]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$A raised D-dimer in a high pre-test probability patient adds little and shouldn't delay definitive imaging.$t$, $t$Malignancy screening is considered after a first unprovoked VTE, since occult cancer is a recognised underlying driver.$t$, $t$DOACs are now first-line over warfarin for most patients. Know the specific exceptions: antiphospholipid syndrome, severe renal impairment, pregnancy.$t$],
  $j$[]$j$::jsonb,
  array[$t$Limb swelling$t$, $t$Limb pain$t$, $t$Breathlessness$t$],
  15
) on conflict (topic_id, slug) do nothing;
